# Line endings, pinned rather than guessed.
#
# Without this, what lands on disk depends on each person's core.autocrlf, and
# two files in here genuinely care:
#
#   *.bat and *.ps1 must be CRLF. Windows PowerShell and cmd both tolerate LF
#   most of the time and then do not - a .bat with LF endings can mis-parse a
#   multi-line block, and that failure looks like the script being wrong rather
#   than the file being wrong.
#
#   The Dockerfile and anything the Linux container runs must be LF. A CRLF
#   shebang line fails with "no such file or directory", naming a file that is
#   plainly there, which is a genuinely horrible hour.
#
# So: LF in the repository for everything, and CRLF on checkout only where
# Windows needs it, whatever anyone's local setting says.

* text=auto eol=lf

*.bat  text eol=crlf
*.ps1  text eol=crlf
*.cmd  text eol=crlf

Dockerfile text eol=lf
*.sh       text eol=lf

# Binary, so Git never tries to "fix" them
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif  binary
*.ico  binary
*.pdf  binary
*.zip  binary
*.xlsx binary

# Generated - keep these out of diffs and out of language statistics
server/package-lock.json linguist-generated=true
web/package-lock.json    linguist-generated=true
tests/package-lock.json  linguist-generated=true
